# Implementation workflow

Do not start implementation because the task looks obvious. Provide a plan first (A–L): current phase, SoT/roadmap requirements, existing code, proposed changes, files to create/modify/avoid, tests, docs, risks, and explicit out-of-scope. **Stop and wait for approval.**

No feature creep: no future-phase features, speculative frameworks, databases, GUIs, auth, or enterprise integrations unless the approved slice requires them. Prefer the smallest implementation that establishes the approved architecture.

After approval, inspect existing headers, sources, CMake, tests, dependencies, and Git history before editing. Do not rewrite working architecture for convenience.

Verification required before calling work done: build, relevant unit/integration tests, regressions, runtime checks where applicable, error paths, scalability where applicable. Compilation alone is not “implemented.”

Use these labels honestly: **IMPLEMENTED**, **TESTED**, **VALIDATED**, **PARTIALLY IMPLEMENTED**, **PLANNED**, **NOT IMPLEMENTED**. In-process/simulation tests are not production proof.

Update only affected docs (`implementation-status.md`, `architecture.md`, `decisions.md`, `roadmap.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`, `docs/README.md`). Never mark future work as implemented. If an ADR is needed, add it. If the SoT must change, **stop**, explain, get approval, edit the Markdown source, regenerate the PDF, and keep the previous PDF in `docs/archive/`.

After implementation, report what was and was not done, files created/modified/deleted, types introduced, architecture/dependency changes, tests run and results, docs, limitations, remaining work, phase status, git status, and a **proposed** commit message. Do not commit.

Never `git commit`, `push`, `reset`, `rebase`, or `merge` without explicit user approval. After approval: add/commit/push only as asked, then verify `git status`, `git log`, and remote tracking. Do not claim push succeeded unless Git confirms it.

A phase is **COMPLETE** only when SoT scope, tests, docs, review, user approval, commit, and confirmed push are all done. Otherwise: **IN PROGRESS**, **PARTIALLY IMPLEMENTED**, **VALIDATED**, or **NOT IMPLEMENTED**.

After a phase: **stop**. Do not start the next phase until the user says so. Verify baseline from Git, code, tests, and docs at the start of every phase — do not trust a previous chat session.
